{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1504354301,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDMyNjc0MDU1MA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1783#issuecomment-326740550"},"message":"Initial rough test of `pypass` on windows:\n- pip install pypass (check)\n- run/import pypass (does not find some executable, presumably gpg)\n- located gpg shipped with git-annex by hand and verified that it is functional (check)\n\nTODOs:\n- we would need to adjust how pypass locates gpg, I guess in the same fashion that we are using the Git that comes with git-annex.\n- we would need to support generating a GPG key, if there is none already -- that would also be beneficial for being able to support annex content encryption more out-of-the-box\n\nOtherwise, pypass seems to give us all features that I can think of.\n- we have completely freedom on where to put the password store and how to structure it\n- there are many pass client available to provide a management UI\n- the password store itself is a git repo (very fitting)","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1504355065,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDMyNjc0MTExMA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1783#issuecomment-326741110"},"message":"How password store is \"unlocked\"?","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1504355366,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDMyNjc0MTMzNA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1783#issuecomment-326741334"},"message":"all gpg","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1504357676,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDMyNjc0MzA3Nw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1783#issuecomment-326743077"},"message":"Think about lame user without gpg agent... Will we need to ask password for every separate invocation of datalad? Or we will use current system to store that one systemwide?","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1504364674,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDMyNjc0OTU4NA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1783#issuecomment-326749584"},"message":"It seems short sighted to draw the line just prior proper gpg support for datalad. That will likewise exclude any data encryption management, and leave that to \"experts\". Especially for a community that both wants to use public services and exchanges sensitive data this seems like a major issue.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1504364739,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDMyNjc0OTY0MQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1783#issuecomment-326749641"},"message":"ah, and to be clear: When we properly support encryption, pypass gives credential management for free.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1504374718,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDMyNjc1OTg1NQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1783#issuecomment-326759855"},"message":"FTR: for a GNOME user unlocking via GPG key or via master password of credential store uses the exact same UI and works out-of-the-box","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1504442762,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDMyNjgwMjgxOQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1783#issuecomment-326802819"},"message":"Update on \"missing executable on windows\":\n\nWhat is missing actually isn't `gpg`. It's `which`!\nSo `import pypass` fails if python is started from a windows shell, while it's working out of the box from within git-bash on windows.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1504444854,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDMyNjgwNDU3OQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1783#issuecomment-326804579"},"message":"Using `pypass` on windows will not easily work.\nIt uses things like `which`, `grep`, etc., suggesting that it will either work in git-bash only or increase dependencies to account for these tools to be available in a regular windows shell.\nFurthermore it depends on `pxssh` from `pexpect` which isn't supported on windows. (In addition the respective import statement used in `pypass` is incorrect)\nSo, we would basically need to fork `pypass` in order to make it work, I guess. Not sure what else would be waiting for us to be dealt with. On the other hand: It's not that big a piece of code, so it might be doable.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1527154957,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM5MTY1NDI0MA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1783#issuecomment-391654240"},"message":"FYI: git for windows comes with a credential manager.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1527165587,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM5MTY5OTU2MA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1783#issuecomment-391699560"},"message":"Keyring module supports extensions, so it should be possible to write backed for that one as well","files":null}]}